[38;5;231m⏺[39m Got it. I'll define the contract as [38;5;153mPOST /login[39m returning [38;5;153m{ userId }[39m, plus [38;5;153mPOST /events[39m, both behind one API client.

  [1mNext question: how long should the logged-in userId last on the client?[0m Other forms will read it later, so this
  decides where they get it from.

  - [1mIn memory only (a module variable):[0m This is the simplest option and leaves nothing in the browser. But a page reload
    or opening a second page loses it. Since the app is one [38;5;153mindex.html[39m today, that's fine for now. Once other forms
    live on other pages, though, you'd have to upgrade, and every reader of the ID would be affected.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m:[0m It survives reloads and navigation within the same tab, and it's cleared when the tab closes. It
    needs a small amount of code for reading safely and clearing on logout. Switching between this and [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m
    later is cheap if the session module is the only thing that touches storage.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m:[0m It survives closing the browser, so it acts like "stay logged in", and it's shared across tabs. It
    also stays on shared machines until someone logs out, and without a server-side session to check against, the stored
    ID can get stale.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, accessed only through a small session module[0m ([38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m, [38;5;153msetUserId()[39m, [38;5;153mclear()[39m).
  Your other forms will likely be on other pages, and this covers that without adding a "remember me" feature nobody
  asked for. Because storage is hidden behind the module, switching to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change.
